WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.266 --> 00:00:08.476
We’ve had to wait a long time to see NASA’s
monster Space Launch System arrive at Launch

00:00:08.476 --> 00:00:12.435
Pad 39B at the agency’s Kennedy Space Center
in Florida.

00:00:12.435 --> 00:00:16.095
The SLS is as famous for its height as it
is for its delays.

00:00:16.095 --> 00:00:21.796
NASA promised to launch the mega-rocket as
early as 2017 only to postpone it again and

00:00:21.796 --> 00:00:22.796
again.

00:00:22.796 --> 00:00:26.376
Its maiden voyage - without a crew aboard
- is now scheduled for this summer.

00:00:26.376 --> 00:00:30.926
Although the SLS is setting the stage for
America’s return to the moon, possibly by

00:00:30.926 --> 00:00:34.466
2025, it’s unlikely to be used for long.

00:00:34.466 --> 00:00:38.995
This may in fact be the beginning of the end
for the SLS.

00:00:38.995 --> 00:00:42.266
The SLS is very, very expensive to fly.

00:00:42.266 --> 00:00:46.736
NASA’s independent watchdog revealed the
cost of launching the SLS and Orion spacecraft

00:00:46.736 --> 00:00:50.216
just once is $4.1 BILLION!

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.286
Inspector General Paul Martin called it “unsustainable”.

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:58.865
That figure, by the way, is eight times higher
than NASA’s estimate of $500 million per

00:00:58.865 --> 00:00:59.865
launch.

00:00:59.865 --> 00:01:04.176
One of the reasons the cost is so high is
that the monster rocket is not reusable.

00:01:04.176 --> 00:01:08.327
After the SLS lifts off and burns through
the fuel in the boosters and core stage, it

00:01:08.327 --> 00:01:12.656
drops them, kind of like how a hiker drops
a heavy backpack to climb the last few miles

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:14.226
to the top of a mountain.

00:01:14.226 --> 00:01:19.306
So why has NASA spent so much to develop a
rocket that can only be used once?

00:01:19.306 --> 00:01:23.546
Former senator Bill Nelson, who now runs NASA
as the administrator, explains that

00:01:23.546 --> 00:01:26.366
"The program is an economic engine for America.

00:01:26.366 --> 00:01:30.976
In 2019 alone, it supported 70,000 good-paying
jobs across the country.”

00:01:30.976 --> 00:01:35.486
Spending huge amounts of money is reminiscent
of the space agency’s operations in the

00:01:35.486 --> 00:01:40.126
60s and 70s when NASA flew 24 astronauts to
the moon as part of the Apollo program.

00:01:40.126 --> 00:01:43.256
Yet the goal at that time was different than
it is now.

00:01:43.256 --> 00:01:48.226
Back then, going to the moon was a political
statement - showing off America’s technological

00:01:48.226 --> 00:01:51.526
might during the Cold War standoff with the
Soviet Union.

00:01:51.526 --> 00:01:55.986
Today, landing on the moon is but the first
step of a bigger plan.

00:01:55.986 --> 00:02:01.006
In 2004, President Bush directed NASA to return
to the moon "with the goal of living and working

00:02:01.006 --> 00:02:04.146
there for increasingly extended periods of
time."

00:02:04.146 --> 00:02:08.756
The aim is establishing a presence on the
moon and eventually, on Mars.

00:02:08.756 --> 00:02:12.256
Whether NASA will help get us there is uncertain.

00:02:12.256 --> 00:02:17.036
Former NASA administrator Charles Bolden predicted
in a 2020 interview with Politico that:

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:22.856
“SLS will go away…It could go away during
a Biden administration or a next Trump administration…

00:02:22.856 --> 00:02:26.446
because at some point commercial entities
are going to catch up.”

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.546
It seems they already have.

00:02:28.546 --> 00:02:32.916
SpaceX is developing its giant Starship to
take people to the moon and Mars.

00:02:32.916 --> 00:02:34.476
Let’s make this real.

00:02:34.476 --> 00:02:37.676
Let’s make this real!

00:02:37.676 --> 00:02:42.296
Starship stands taller than any of the planned
SLS models beginning with the initial configuration,

00:02:42.296 --> 00:02:44.976
Block 1 Cargo, ending with the Block 2 Cargo.

00:02:44.976 --> 00:02:49.256
It also touts the ability to send more tons
to low-Earth orbit.

00:02:49.256 --> 00:02:53.476
Specs aside, what really sets the launch vehicles
apart is their price tag.

00:02:53.476 --> 00:02:59.016
SpaceX CEO Elon Musk has said Starship could
eventually fly for just $2 million per mission.

00:02:59.016 --> 00:03:01.746
As opposed to the SLS’ $4 billion.

00:03:01.746 --> 00:03:06.856
Even if it costs more than $2 million it will
still be far less than the SLS because Starship

00:03:06.856 --> 00:03:07.856
is reusable.

00:03:07.856 --> 00:03:12.586
Both the first-stage booster, the Super Heavy,
and the upper-stage spacecraft Starship can

00:03:12.586 --> 00:03:14.436
return to earth and fly again.

00:03:14.436 --> 00:03:18.496
NASA was so impressed with SpaceX that it
has chosen Starship to help with the final

00:03:18.496 --> 00:03:20.216
leg of the journey to the moon.

00:03:20.216 --> 00:03:25.226
A modified version of Starship will help land
the first woman and next man on the lunar

00:03:25.226 --> 00:03:26.226
surface.

00:03:26.226 --> 00:03:29.936
The journey would begin on Earth with the
SLS which lifts the Orion spacecraft carrying

00:03:29.936 --> 00:03:33.986
astronauts on a path to the moon, helping
it escape Earth’s gravity.

00:03:33.986 --> 00:03:38.746
Then, Orion would meet up with a modified,
lunar variant of Starship in lunar orbit.

00:03:38.746 --> 00:03:42.916
The crew would transfer to Starship which
would then take them

00:03:42.916 --> 00:03:47.596
For the trip home, Starship lifts off, meets
up with Orion which was left in lunar orbit,

00:03:47.596 --> 00:03:50.636
the crew transfers over to Orion, and returns
to Earth.

00:03:50.636 --> 00:03:55.276
But what if Starship was used to go all the
way to the moon, without the need for the

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:57.186
SLS or Orion?

00:03:57.186 --> 00:04:00.856
Starship could launch, meet up with another
starship to refuel, before journeying to the

00:04:00.856 --> 00:04:01.856
moon.

00:04:01.856 --> 00:04:05.496
This version of Starship is not the lunar
model - it still has the heat shield and flaps

00:04:05.496 --> 00:04:07.405
for landing back on earth.

00:04:07.405 --> 00:04:11.066
SpaceX isn’t the only commercial company
with big plans for space.

00:04:11.066 --> 00:04:15.635
Blue Origin is getting another shot at securing
a NASA moon landing contract after initially

00:04:15.635 --> 00:04:17.386
losing out to SpaceX.

00:04:17.386 --> 00:04:21.846
Jeff Bezos’ space venture is also reportedly
trying to make its heavy-lift launch vehicle

00:04:21.846 --> 00:04:25.036
New Glenn fully reusable like Starship.

00:04:25.036 --> 00:04:27.895
So NASA does appear to have private options
to lean on.

00:04:27.895 --> 00:04:32.766
In many ways, the SLS is a final hurrah for
NASA, paying homage to the days of the past.

00:04:32.766 --> 00:04:37.355
It uses the main engines from the space shuttle.

00:04:37.355 --> 00:04:42.365
Its twin solid rocket boosters are built on
a foundation of knowledge and experience gained

00:04:42.365 --> 00:04:44.936
from developing the space shuttle boosters.

00:04:44.936 --> 00:04:49.145
Once SpaceX and others test and fly their
reusable rockets successfully, the game will

00:04:49.145 --> 00:04:50.615
change forever.

00:04:50.615 --> 00:04:55.906
Opening up an era of space travel where trips
to space could be as frequent as planes fly.

